I'm not so sure; the Wizard has a lot of late-game advantages from his
spells and curse absorption.  But for the sake of the rest of the
discussion, I'll concede "In the top 3" (the other two being Valk and
Barb) and we can go from there.

>> [advantages snipped]    If they can survive the early game (which
>> is just as much of a challenge for all other melee-weak characters),
>> they should ascend in a breeze, barring poor/stupid play.
> 
> But isn't that the key statement; hard characters are the ones who
> have difficulties surviving the _early game_ (later things do not
> differ so much any more between the classes). 

Not always true.  A monk's real problem starts in the mid to late game,
when his inability to wear body armor costs him either his amulet slot
or forces him to wear a shield for reflection, ...and also costs him
some key points of AC no matter what.

Late game non-spellcasters (barb, cave) have a definite disadvantage
compared to a wizard if they haven't found the appropriate
wands/scrolls/armor (jumping boots, anyone?) to compensate for not
having the spells.  There's a huge difference between having to wade
through Astral blow-by-blow and being able to jump past most of it.

A wizard who survives to get Magicbane, on the other hand, should
ascend, barring extraordinary circumstances.

>> However, this also assumes an experienced player.  A new player will
>> spend their time either casting force bolt for combat (and dying),
> 
> Actually, my current Wizard is casting force bolt like mad; and is
> not dying.[*] (He is also engraving a lot the E-word with Magicbane
> to survive; it *is* an advantage for Wizards to find an early altar!)

And just how effective would you say casting force bolt is when you
DON'T have a permanent, infinite source of one-turn Elbereth that you
got from a fortunate early altar?

- From watching plenty of early-game wizards fail on NAO by trying to do
just that, I can tell you: not very.  It misses.  A lot.

>> So yes, I
>> am perfectly willing to believe that Wizards have the lowest ascension
>> rate in the game.  I think it comes from most people expecting Wizards
>> to be something entirely different than they actually are, and trying to
>> make the game conform to their expectations instead of vice versa.

Oh, and I left one out earlier -- a lot of people play wizards for the
"cool" starting magic items, and try to solve the early-game
difficulties by sticking their faces in all the nearest fountains.  That
leads to a lot of deaths as well that aren't attributable to quit-scumming.

> [*] Besides the staff (that you don't want to enhance further) force
> bolt is the only attack means that you start with and that you can use
> from the beginning; and that you also want to enhance since you need
> attack spells all the way until the endgame.

Thrown daggers from the Mines and your pet with dust Elbereth, etc.  I'm
sure someone will chime in with an all-ranged, all-the-time spiel soon;
and for Wizards, it's actually an accurate path to ascension.  Besides,
trying to kill everything in the early game is one of the mistakes
early-game wizards make.

